steps.body:
When something goes wrong at run time, JavaScript *throws* an error. If nothing catches it, the program stops on that line, Node prints a stack trace to stderr, and the program exits with a failure code. Wrap risky code in `try` and handle the failure in `catch`:

```javascript
const text = "{ not valid json";
try {
  const data = JSON.parse(text);
  console.log(data.user.name);
} catch (error) {
  console.log(`caught a ${error.name}`); // caught a SyntaxError
}
console.log("still running");
```

When a line inside `try` throws, the rest of the block is skipped and `catch` runs with the error object. After the catch block the program carries on as normal. Every error has `error.name` (its type) and `error.message` (the details).

The built-in errors you will meet most:

- `SyntaxError`: `JSON.parse` got text that is not valid JSON.
- `TypeError`: reading a property of `null` or `undefined`, or calling something that is not a function.
- `RangeError`: a value outside the allowed range, such as `"ab".repeat(-1)`.
- `ReferenceError`: using a name that was never declared.

A typo in your own code is a SyntaxError too, but Node finds it before the program starts, so no `try` can catch it. Print `error.name` for built-in errors, not `error.message`: Node's wording can change between versions. Keep each `try` block small so you know which line it guards.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const lines = input.trim().split("\n");

for (const line of lines) {
  try {
    const data = JSON.parse(line);
    console.log(`name: ${data.user.name}`);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`error: ${error.name}`);
  }
}
console.log("done");

