}
console.log(`good: ${good}, bad: ${bad}`);

steps.body:
You can throw errors yourself with `throw`. Pick the error type that says what went wrong and give it a clear message:

```javascript
function setVolume(level) {
  if (typeof level !== "number" || Number.isNaN(level)) {
    throw new TypeError("level must be a number");
  }
  if (level < 0 || level > 10) {
    throw new RangeError("level must be 0 to 10");
  }
  return level;
}
console.log(setVolume(7)); // 7
```

- `new Error(message)` is the general kind.
- `new TypeError(message)` means the value has the wrong type.
- `new RangeError(message)` means the type is right but the value is out of range.

Validate arguments at the top of a function, before any real work. The rest of the function can then trust its inputs, and callers get a clear failure instead of a wrong answer. `throw` leaves the function at once, like `return`, and the nearest `catch` up the call chain receives the error.

Watch out: `Number("abc")` is `NaN`, and `typeof NaN` is still `"number"`, so a type check needs `Number.isNaN` too. Messages you wrote yourself never change, so printing `${error.name}: ${error.message}` is safe. Always throw an `Error` object, never a bare string: a string has no name or message.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const [startText, ...amounts] = input.trim().split("\n");

function withdraw(balance, amount) {
  if (typeof amount !== "number" || Number.isNaN(amount)) {
    throw new TypeError("amount must be a number");
  }
  if (amount <= 0) {
    throw new RangeError("amount must be positive");
  }
  if (amount > balance) {
    throw new RangeError("insufficient funds");
  }
  return balance - amount;
}

let balance = Number(startText);
for (const text of amounts) {
  try {
    balance = withdraw(balance, Number(text));
    console.log(`balance: ${balance}`);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`${error.name}: ${error.message}`);
  }
}

